# Rules for the UI Kit Project

## File Structure

- Components must be in the `lib/` directory
- Each component must have its own folder and include:
  - The component file
  - A story file
  - A unit test file

## Naming Conventions

- Components should use PascalCase (e.g., Button.tsx)
- Story files should follow the pattern ComponentName.stories.tsx
- Interfaces should use PascalCase and start with 'I' (e.g., IButtonProps)
- Variables and functions should use camelCase

## React Components

- Keep components functional and small
- Define TypeScript types for all props
- Document important props using JSDoc
- For more complex components, create partials in a `partials/` sub-directory
- Use forwardRef for components that require refs

## React Aria

- Use React Aria Components (RAC) as the base for components (e.g., use RACButton as the base for Button)
- Extend React Aria prop interfaces (e.g., ButtonProps extends RACButtonProps)
- Use composeRenderProps to combine custom classes with React Aria states
- Spread all props into the base React Aria component
- Leverage renderProps states like isPressed, isFocused, etc.
- Name imported React Aria components with the RAC prefix for clarity (e.g., Button as RACButton)
- Follow the implementation guides for each React Aria component
- Use React Aria hooks to implement accessible behaviors when needed
- Import React Aria components and hooks selectively to optimize bundle size

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Keep Tailwind classes organized and reusable
- Use tailwind-variants (tv) to manage component states and variants
- Define custom variants that complement React Aria functionality
- Use utilities like focusRing for a consistent user experience
- Provide default variant values using defaultVariants in tv()
- Avoid inline styles

## Testing

- Write unit tests for components
- Test files should follow the pattern ComponentName.spec.tsx
- Use Vitest for testing
- Maintain good test coverage

## Documentation

- Document important props with JSDoc
- Keep README.md up to date
- Provide usage examples in Storybook stories

## Git

- Make atomic commits
- Use descriptive commit messages
- Keep .gitignore updated

## TypeScript

- Use strict types
- Avoid `any`
- Define interfaces for all props
- Use type inference when possible

## Accessibility

- Include ARIA attributes when needed
- Ensure proper color contrast
- Ensure components are keyboard-navigable

## Performance

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use React.memo when appropriate
- Optimize imports

## Dependencies

- Use yarn to install dependencies
- Keep React Aria dependencies up to date

## Implementations

- Implementations must pass linting
